<%# Step one: which account.

    Links rather than a <select> + submit, because each choice carries a figure the reader needs in
    order to make it ("acme · 18 repositories"), and a select can only show one at a time. The
    target is a plain GET with `?organization=`, so every choice is linkable and the back button
    lands here rather than in a half-filled form. That parameter keeps its name deliberately even
    though it now carries personal logins too — see new.html.erb. %>
<div class="space-y-3">
  <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
    Accounts with repositories the SpecGuard GitHub App is installed on that GitHub lists you as an
    administrator of. Your own account is listed here too when you administer repositories in it.
  </p>

  <div class="grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-2">
    <% organizations.each do |org| %>
      <%= render UI::CardComponent.new(href: bulk_repositories_path(organization: org.login)) do %>
        <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-3">
          <%= render UI::HeadingComponent.new(level: 3, class: "text-app-content min-w-0 break-words") { org.login } %>
          <div class="flex shrink-0 items-center gap-1">
            <%# Only the personal case is marked. An organization card carries no counterpart badge
                — the page's own wording already says organizations are here, and marking the
                majority to distinguish the minority is noise on every card.

                `personal?` is a positive claim rather than `!organization?`, and that distinction
                is the whole reason `Org` has two predicates: GitHub may report no owner type at
                all, and marking on the negation would print "Personal" over a namespace GitHub
                never described. Such a card is offered like any other and simply carries no
                marker — withholding rather than inventing. %>
            <% if org.personal? %>
              <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :neutral) { "Personal" } %>
            <% end %>
            <%= render UI::BadgeComponent.new(tone: :success) do %>
              <%= pluralize(org.administered_count, "repository", plural: "repositories") %>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </div>
        <%# Stated per organization rather than once for the page, because it is a fact about THIS
            organization: the reader who knows acme has thirty repositories connected needs to know
            why three are on offer, and the answer is different for every card. %>
        <% if (withheld = withheld_repositories_sentence(org.withheld_count)) %>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-app-muted"><%= withheld %></p>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# The listing is capped (GithubApi::MAX_PAGES), and the cap is GLOBAL rather than per
      account — so a truncated listing can hide a whole account, not merely some of one account's
      repositories. That is a different and worse failure than a short list, and the page says so
      rather than presenting a partial set of accounts as the complete set. %>
  <% if github_listing.truncated? %>
    <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, class: "mt-2") do %>
      GitHub returned more repositories than SpecGuard reads in one pass, so this list may be
      missing an account. Repositories can always be registered one at a time.
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
